# AI Quality Control

## Objective

Treat AI-generated code as a draft that needs verification, not a finished deliverable by default.

## Core Principles

- Run or mentally trace the code path before claiming it works.
- Cross-check generated code against actual project files, not assumptions.
- Flag any part of the output that is uncertain or unverified.
- Don't claim tests pass, a build succeeds, or a bug is fixed without actually verifying it (running it, or clearly stating it wasn't run).

## Requirements

- When a runtime/tool is available, actually execute tests/builds instead of asserting they'd pass.
- When verification isn't possible in the current environment, say so explicitly rather than implying it was checked.
- Re-read requirements against the final output line by line before calling it complete.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Stating 'this fixes the bug' without having reproduced or verified the fix.
- Claiming full test coverage without having actually run the tests.
- Silently dropping a requirement that turned out to be inconvenient to implement.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ Every completion claim ('fixed', 'tested', 'works') is backed by an actual check, not an assumption.
✓ Unverifiable claims are explicitly labeled as unverified.
✓ The final output was checked against the original requirement list.

## Golden Rule

'It should work' is not the same as 'I verified it works' — say which one is true.
